Documentation programs
======================

The .cpp files in this directory exist to PRODUCE FIGURES OF THE GENEVA MANUAL
(suite Invariant 27; rule D2 in the manual repository's
DOCUMENTATION-INVARIANTS.md). Every program here

  * compiles and links against this library's own dependency closure only,
  * runs as an S-size ctest case, so it cannot rot without turning a gate red,
  * writes its output to the WORKING DIRECTORY -- never into the source tree --
    so it can be run from a build tree by the manual's `make figures` target.

They are deliberately separate from examples/: an example teaches a user how to
use the library, a documentation program backs a specific listing or figure in
the manual.

Figure producers
----------------

plot_rng_distributions  Draws 6000 numbers from each of eight distributions
                        through a proxy and writes one ROOT macro per
                        distribution, a histogram of its draws with a legend
                        that states its parameters:
                          rng-even.C        double, uniform on [0, 1)
                          rng-evenwb.C      double, uniform on [-3, 2)
                          rng-discrete.C    int32_t, uniform on [0, 10]
                          rng-discretewb.C  int32_t, uniform on [-3, 10]
                          rng-gauss.C       one Gaussian, mean 0, sigma 0.5
                          rng-dgauss.C      bi_normal_distribution: two peaks
                                            of sigma 0.5 at distance 2 about
                                            mean 0
                          rng-bitsimple.C   boolean, p(true) = 0.5
                          rng-bitprob.C     boolean, p(true) = 0.7
                        Each macro builds the canvas cc and saves nothing; the
                        renderer prints it. The legends are formatted from the
                        distribution objects the program draws with, and the
                        program fails if a draw contradicts the range it states
                        (half-open for doubles, closed for integers). Hap's
                        random numbers are not reproducible from a seed
                        (DEVELOPMENT-INVARIANTS.md, Invariant 18), so a rerun
                        draws different but equivalent histograms.
